    "Functional groups matter because they're the 'reactive part' of an otherwise unreactive carbon skeleton. A long hydrocarbon chain is fairly inert; swap one hydrogen for -OH and you get an alcohol with completely different properties (it can react with sodium, it can be oxidised to an acid). This is why homologous series work: every member shares the same functional group and so reacts the same way, just scaled up by more -CH2- units.",
    "Saturation explains the flame test and the addition-vs-substitution split. Saturated hydrocarbons (single bonds only, alkanes) burn with a clean blue flame because complete combustion is easy; unsaturated ones (double/triple bonds, alkenes/alkynes) burn yellow and sooty because the extra bonds mean incomplete combustion leaves carbon behind. Those same extra bonds are also *why* unsaturated molecules undergo addition reactions (like hydrogenation turning oil into vanaspati) — there's a bond sitting there ready to be opened up.",
