    "Mirror and lens formulas look intimidating but both describe the same relationship — where an object is versus where its image forms — with the sign convention doing the real work of telling you whether that image is real or virtual, upright or inverted, in front of or behind the mirror/lens. Once you're comfortable with the convention (real is positive for lenses, object distance is always negative), image prediction becomes mechanical rather than something to memorise case by case.",
    "A concave mirror's dual personality (virtual/magnified when the object is close, real/inverted when it's far) comes from the same converging behaviour viewed at different distances — inside the focal length, the reflected rays haven't converged yet so they only appear to meet behind the mirror (virtual); outside it, they actually cross in front of the mirror (real). A convex mirror never has this split personality because it's always diverging light, which is exactly why it's chosen for rear-view mirrors — it always gives a smaller, right-side-up, wide-field image no matter the distance.",
